Not necessarily so. I think they have learned to coexist after many years of competition.
Do the consumers in Japan usually buy food at local supermarkets?
Yes. But in smaller cities and even in the old downtown area of Tokyo, we have a lot of small independent specialized retail stores.
In case we have plans to open a hypermarket chain in Japan, could we place our orders directly with major manufacturers?
I don't think so. You'd have to do business with some of the major wholesalers.
No particular reason. It's just custom. You'd have to book your order with a wholesaler even if the goods come directly from your manufacturer.
Most Japanese business companies normally follow two ways to find new employees, recruiting from schools and advertising in print media. It seems that the latter is often very effective.
Schools are closed for five-six weeks. Government offices remain open but their employees take several days off by turns. Businesses do about the same way, thought some companies are closed for a week or so.
